.TH DUCKNETVIEW 1 "2026-10" "ducknetview 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ducknetview \- terminal network monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ducknetview
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ducknetview \-\-diff
.I old.json new.json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ducknetview
is a terminal user interface for watching a host's network: interfaces and their
throughput, listening sockets, processes, active and recently closed
connections, a log of what changed, and the host's routing configuration.
.PP
On Linux, per-connection throughput, round-trip time and retransmit counts are
read from the kernel's
.B sock_diag
netlink interface \(em the same source
.BR ss (8)
uses. No elevated privileges are required. On other platforms those columns are
hidden rather than shown empty. The same dump gives each TCP listener's accept
queue (the Ports tab's QUEUE column, as waiting/backlog), and the Overview's
Host TCP panel reads the host\-wide counters in
.I /proc/net/snmp
and
.IR /proc/net/netstat :
listen overflows and drops, SYN cookies, retransmits, timeouts, bad segments
and resets, as per\-minute rates.
.PP
On Linux, sockets in other network namespaces \(em containers on bridge
networks, and namespaces made with
.BR ip\-netns (8)
\(em are enumerated through
.IR /proc/<pid>/net ,
one member process per namespace, and labelled in a NETNS column (container id,
.B ip netns
name, or
.BR netns:<inode> ;
.B host
for ducknetview's own) that appears when more than one namespace is present and
is matched by search. A container's wildcard listener is classed
.B netns
rather than exposed unless a
.B docker\-proxy
on the host publishes it. Unprivileged, other users' namespaces cannot be read,
and byte counters inside a namespace need root (they are read after
.BR setns (2));
either limitation is logged once per session as a
.B netns_limited
event. At most 32 namespaces are scanned per poll.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-once
Print a one-shot text snapshot and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a fuller snapshot as JSON and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " file"
Browse a
.B \-\-json
snapshot instead of the live host.
.B \-
reads standard input, so
.RB ` "ssh host ducknetview --json | ducknetview --from -" '
browses a remote host. Given
.BI ssh:// [user@]host
instead of a file, the snapshot is re-fetched over
.BR ssh (1)
on the refresh interval, which makes the view live: two consecutive fetches
carry the same counters a local poll would, so throughput, totals, events and
the alert rules all work on the remote host. Nothing needs to be installed
there beyond the binary itself.
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Compare the two
.B \-\-json
snapshots named as arguments and exit, reporting the listeners, processes,
remote hosts and interfaces that differ. Exits
.B 0
when they match and
.B 1
when they do not, like
.BR diff (1).
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Run headless: poll, apply every alert rule, record the daily usage and write
events to standard output, with no interface. This is the shape the alerting
wants — the moment nobody is watching is the moment it matters. Combine it
with
.BR \-\-log\-events ,
.B \-\-on\-alert
and
.BR \-\-metrics\-listen .
It stops on
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
persisting the session's totals first, and re-reads its configuration on
.B SIGHUP
without losing them: a restart to pick up a new threshold would throw away the
session totals, the event ring and the rate baselines, which are most of what a
long-running daemon accumulates. A reload is itself recorded as a
.B config_reloaded
event, at warning level when part of the new configuration had to be ignored.
.IP
The first poll of a session reports nothing as new, by design. When a listener
baseline is saved and listeners are already outside it, that poll instead
raises one alert-level
.B baseline_drift
event naming them, so a daemon restarted onto a drifted host still fires
.BR \-\-on\-alert .
Listener and per-process throughput events name the owning systemd unit
beside the PID, which is what a service's operator knows it by.
.TP
.B \-\-metrics
Print the host's network state in Prometheus text format and exit, for a
node_exporter textfile directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-metrics\-listen " address"
Serve the same metrics over HTTP at
.I address
(for example
.BR :9847 )
under
.BR /metrics ,
so Prometheus can scrape directly rather than reading a textfile. It runs
alongside the TUI or the daemon, and is answered from that session's own state
rather than by sampling the host again — which also exports what only a running
session knows: how long since the last completed poll, how many polls and
events there have been, the session's accumulated byte totals, and how many
alerts and failed hooks. The poll age is the one metric that can reveal a
monitor which is up but no longer working. Scrapes are cached for five seconds
and, if the session cannot answer within two, fall back to sampling. Note that
this binds a listening socket: give it an explicit address such as
.B 127.0.0.1:9847
unless the exporter really should be reachable from the network.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " n"
Print the
.I n
busiest processes and hosts and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-usage " days"
Print the recorded daily usage for the last
.I days
days and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-usage\-all
Print every recorded day of usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-baseline
Compare the host's listeners against the saved baseline. Exits
.B 0
when clean,
.B 1
when unexpected listeners are present, and
.B 2
when no baseline has been saved.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Report everything wrong with the saved configuration and exit. The loader is
deliberately forgiving — an out-of-range value falls back to its default and a
malformed one is skipped, so that a hand-edited file can never wedge the
interface — and the cost of that forgiveness is silence. A watchlist regular
expression that does not compile, a latency target with no port, a hidden
column key that names nothing, a binding for an action that does not exist and
a misspelt key all simply do nothing, for ever. This reports all of them at
once. Exits
.B 0
when the config is entirely usable,
.B 1
when anything in it is being ignored, and
.B 2
when the file could not be read or parsed at all. Problems that disable
nothing are printed as warnings and do not change the exit status: a key that
differs from the documented one only in case (it is applied, since keys match
case\-insensitively) and a latency target that does not resolve at the moment.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " file"
Check against a baseline file rather than the per-user one.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-baseline " file"
Write the current listeners to a portable baseline file and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-events " file"
Append events to a file as JSON lines. The file is rotated at 8 MiB, keeping one
previous generation.
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-alert " command"
Run
.I command
for each alert-level event. It receives the event kind, subject and detail as
arguments, and
.BR DNV_EVENT_KIND ,
.BR DNV_EVENT_LEVEL ,
.BR DNV_EVENT_SUBJECT ,
.BR DNV_EVENT_DETAIL
and
.B DNV_EVENT_AT
in its environment. It is executed directly, never through a shell.
.TP
.BI \-\-tab " name"
Open on a particular tab: overview, interfaces, ports, processes, connections,
events or routes (also procs, conns and neighbours).
.TP
.BI \-\-view " name"
Open in a saved view: the whole arrangement of the tables — tab, sort, search,
quick filters and toggles — under a name, as saved from the
.B V
overlay. With an unknown name the saved names are listed; an explicitly empty
name
.RB ( \-\-view= )
prints the saved names one per line and exits. Applied before
.BR \-\-tab ,
so the two compose.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the option list and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B 1\-7
Jump to a tab.
.B ?
shows the full key list, which is authoritative; the bindings below are the ones
worth knowing first.
.TP
.B /
Search. Prefix the query with
.B re:
for a regular expression;
.B \e
switches between filtering rows and highlighting them, and
.BR n / N
jump between matches.
.TP
.B t
Switch the throughput columns between current rate and session total.
.B U
switches between bytes and bits per second.
.TP
.B z
On the Connections tab, show connections that have closed.
.B g
groups the live connections by remote host and
.B i
filters them by interface.
.TP
.B b
Save the current listeners as the expected baseline;
.B a
accepts one highlighted listener into it;
.B B
clears it.
.TP
.B w
Add the highlighted connection's remote to the watchlist.
.TP
.B F
Follow the highlighted process or listener into the connections it accounts
for, and press it again on the Connections tab to go back.
.TP
.B T
On the Processes tab, arrange the rows as a tree of what started what.
.B A
folds rows sharing a name instead; the two are exclusive.
.TP
.B ,
Open the settings overlay: the alert thresholds, the latency targets, the
hidden columns and the theme, all of which previously had to be hand-edited in
the config file. Changes are written out as they are made.
.TP
.B V
Open the views overlay: named arrangements of the tables.
.B enter
applies the highlighted one,
.B n
saves the current screen under a name,
.B d
deletes one. A view records the tab, the per-tab searches, every sort column
and direction, the quick filters and the view toggles — everything it takes to
get back to a particular question. It does not record a drill, which points at
PIDs that will not exist next time.
.TP
.B x
Signal the highlighted row's process, confirming with
.BR t / k / h
for TERM, KILL or HUP.
.TP
.B q
Quit (with a confirmation).
.B Ctrl\-C
quits immediately.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/ducknetview/config.json
Settings, the watchlist, the listener baseline and the alert thresholds. The
directory is the platform's user config directory, so on macOS it is under
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support .
A
.B \-\-daemon
session never writes this file.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/ducknetview/usage.json
Recorded daily traffic totals, flushed every five minutes and on exit.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/ducknetview/trend.json
A day of per\-minute throughput peaks, so the Overview can show what happened
before the session started.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Almost every setting is written to the config file as it is changed in the TUI
and restored on the next launch: the sort columns and directions, the quick
filters, the hide\-noise / reverse\-DNS / grouping / totals / aggregate toggles,
the rate unit, the search mode, the refresh interval, the last open tab, the
watchlist and the listener baseline. Unknown keys are ignored and out\-of\-range
values fall back to the default, so an old or hand\-mangled file cannot wedge the
UI.
.PP
The thresholds, the latency targets, the hidden columns and the theme are all
editable from the settings overlay
.RB ( , ),
which writes this file as changes are made. They can equally be edited by hand:
.TP
.B hidden_columns
An array of column keys to leave out of the tables, for narrow terminals. A
hidden column stays sortable with
.BR s .
Valid keys: age, closed, conns, container, count, cpu, host, iface, life,
listen, local, mem, name, netns, owner, pid, process, procs, proto, queue, remote,
rtt, rtx, rx, state, states, svc, tx, unit, user. Hiding every column of a table is ignored.
.TP
.B latency_targets
An array of extra
.IR host : port
probes for the Overview's latency panel. The default gateway is always probed;
anything listed here may leave the LAN, which is why it is opt\-in.
.TP
.B watchlist
An array of CIDRs, bare IPs or regular expressions. Each is tested against the
remote IP and its resolved hostname; matches are highlighted and logged as
events.
.B w
adds and removes the highlighted remote.
.TP
.B "alert_process_bps, alert_connection_bps, alert_rtt_ms, alert_retrans_per_min, alert_conn_growth_polls, alert_fanout_hosts, alert_iface_drops_per_min, alert_listen_queue_pct, alert_listen_drops_per_min, alert_close_wait"
The alert thresholds: bytes per second for a process or a single connection,
smoothed round\-trip time in milliseconds, retransmits per minute on one socket,
consecutive polls of connection growth before calling it a leak, new remote
hosts in one poll before calling it fan\-out, errors plus drops per minute
on one interface, a TCP listener's accept queue as a percentage of its backlog,
connections the host drops on the listen path per minute, and sockets in
CLOSE_WAIT held by one process. Each is off when absent or
.BR 0 ,
since the right number is site\-specific, and each re\-fires at most once a
minute per subject.
.TP
.B asn_database
Path to an ip2asn table (the five\-column TSV published by iptoasn.com, plain
or gzipped). With one, an OWNER column appears on the Connections tab and the
detail overlay names the autonomous system behind each public address. Nothing
is bundled and no lookup leaves the host; without the key the column simply
does not exist.
.TP
.B theme
Colour palette:
.BR default ,
.B contrast
or
.BR mono .
.B NO_COLOR
still overrides all of them.
.TP
.B keys
Key bindings, as action name to key — for example
.BR "\(dqclosed\(dq: \(dqZ\(dq" .
An unknown action, a reserved key (ctrl\-c, tab, the arrows, the digits) or a
key already taken by another action is ignored rather than obeyed, so a bad
config cannot leave the tool unusable. The help overlay renders what is
actually bound.
.TP
.B process_tree
Whether the Processes tab opens as a tree.
.TP
.B views
The saved views, as written by the
.B V
overlay. A view with no name, a duplicate name, or one past the thirty-second
is dropped.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour entirely.
.SH METRICS
Both metric modes export the host's state:
.BR ducknetview_iface_receive_bytes_per_second ,
.BR ducknetview_iface_transmit_bytes_per_second ,
.BR ducknetview_iface_errors_total ,
.BR ducknetview_iface_up ,
.BR ducknetview_listeners ,
.BR ducknetview_listeners_off_baseline " (\-1 when no baseline is saved, which is not the same as clean),"
.BR ducknetview_connections ,
.BR ducknetview_connection_retransmits_total ,
.BR ducknetview_processes_with_sockets ,
and on Linux
.BR ducknetview_listener_queue_ratio ,
.B ducknetview_listener_accept_queue
and
.BR ducknetview_tcp_counter_total .
.PP
A scrape answered by a running session adds what only it can report:
.BR ducknetview_build_info ,
.BR ducknetview_session_uptime_seconds ,
.BR ducknetview_last_poll_age_seconds ,
.BR ducknetview_polls_total ,
.BR ducknetview_paused ,
.BR ducknetview_session_bytes_total ,
.BR ducknetview_events_total ,
.BR ducknetview_alerts_total ,
.B ducknetview_alert_hook_failures_total
and, for a
.B \-\-from ssh://
session,
.BR ducknetview_remote_up .
.PP
.B ducknetview_last_poll_age_seconds
is the one worth alerting on first: it is \-1 before the first poll and grows
without bound if the session stops working, which no other metric here reveals.
The README documents the labels and the event kinds that
.B ducknetview_events_total
is broken down by.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration without restarting. The session's totals, event ring
and rate baselines are kept.
.TP
.B SIGINT ", " SIGTERM
Flush the usage totals counted since the last periodic flush and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
when
.B \-\-check\-baseline
finds unexpected listeners,
.B \-\-check\-config
finds problems or
.B \-\-diff
finds changes, and
.B 2
for a usage or collection error (an unknown
.B \-\-tab
or
.BR \-\-view ,
an unexpected argument, a host that cannot be read).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ss (8),
.BR ip (8),
.BR nethogs (8)
